{{#extend "layout" showSource=true}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Recipe &middot; controllers</p>
    <h1>Controllers return; they do not mutate</h1>
    <p>
      A controller that changed <code>options.model</code> in place was fine in v1 and is
      usually fine in v2 — until the model is a plain object and the site is running under
      <code>.watch()</code>. Then it drifts, one edit at a time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Why the model kind matters</h3>
      <p>
        A <code>.json</code> file, a models folder or an <code>http(s)://</code> model is
        re-resolved on every build, so an in-place change is thrown away with it. A
        <strong>plain object</strong> model is replayed from a shallow snapshot of the original
        <code>.page()</code> call — the same object, every rebuild.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>What that looks like</h3>
      <p>
        A controller doing <code>model.pours.push(&hellip;)</code> adds one more row on every
        save. The dev server looks fine at first and drifts further from a fresh build with
        each edit, which is the worst kind of bug to be told about second-hand.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>This page's controller</h3>
      <p>
        It numbers the pours and counts them by building a new object:
        <code>&#123; ...model, pours: model.pours.map(&hellip;), count &#125;</code>. Nothing it
        was handed is touched, so the tenth rebuild renders exactly what the first one did.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>{{model.count}} brew recipes, numbered by the controller</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>step</th><th>method</th><th>ratio</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {{#each model.pours}}
        <tr><td>{{step}}</td><td>{{name}}</td><td>{{ratio}}</td></tr>
        {{/each}}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="meta">Run the example with <code>--dev</code>, edit a page, and the count stays 2. Under the v1 habit it would be 3, then 4.</p>
  </div>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "demo"}}
  <p>
    The same rule covers <code>options.config</code>: every page renders with its own shallow
    copy, so a controller may override config for its own page, but nested objects such as
    <code>folders</code> are shared and must be left alone. Return new values and the
    distinction never has to be remembered.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
